  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Shower and thunderstorm chances increase for the \nfirst half of next week with the potential for areas of heavy rain \nand some strong thunderstorms.\n\nAs a strong upper-level ridge expands into the Plains early next \nweek, northwesterly flow aloft will occur across much of the East. \nThis ridge gets pushed back some as a potentially strong upper-level \ntrough in eastern Canada sends spokes of energy southeastward across \nthe Great Lakes and Northeast. This will initially allow for the \nupper-level trough to become more carved out across the Northeast by \nlater this weekend. Some guidance maintains a rather vigorous \nshortwave sliding southeastward across the Great Lakes Monday and \nthen across the Northeast during Tuesday. This results in some model \nsolutions closing off the trough across the Northeast Tuesday into \nWednesday. Some other guidance is slower and thus sharpens or closes \noff the trough a day or two later. The upper-level system will drive \nsurface low pressure and a cold front our way Tuesday into \nWednesday. \n\nWhat all this boils down to is that it looks like we start to turn \nconvectively active by Monday, with the most active day likely to be \nTuesday, and then convection starting to decrease in activity \nWednesday. While there remains some differences among the model \nguidance on the timing and strength of the features diving \nsoutheastward early next week, the setup would tend to favor \nupstream convective development given northwesterly flow aloft. This \nwould be enhanced if the trough aloft closes off. Deeper moisture \n(dew points into the low 70s) advecting northward ahead of this \nfeature with eventual cooling aloft and stronger forcing for ascent \nmay result in widespread showers and some thunderstorms. Depending \non the evolution of this system, some areas of heavy rainfall and \nstrong thunderstorms are possible. The intensity and coverage of \nthunderstorms though may ultimately depend on the prior days \nconvective development and then its evolution downstream.",
